---
/** Public projection of Station's declared privacy inventory.
 * Corpus checks bind reviewed upstream inputs and this page's bytes.
 * Source review does not establish completeness or deployment.
 */
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '@/components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '@/components/Footer.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import Eyebrow from '@/components/Eyebrow.astro';

const updated = '5 November 2026';
const inventoryFingerprint = 'ad8af6b4ff8a8727';
const policyCommit = '083dea55cbbe6e8a30c031fb1d28a9f3c2efdbff';
const policyUrl = `https://github.com/kontourai/station/blob/${policyCommit}/docs/privacy-policy.md`;
---

<Layout
  title="Station app privacy — what the app collects, in plain terms · Kontour AI"
  description="What the Station app collects, what leaves your machine and what turns it on, which permissions exist and why, and how to get your data deleted."
>
  <Nav slot="header" />

  <Section contour size="hero" borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <Eyebrow accent="gold" gap="sm">Station app privacy</Eyebrow>
      <h1 class="display-lg privacy-title">What the app collects.</h1>
      <p class="lead measure privacy-lead">
        Station runs on hardware you or your operator control. The short version:
        networked features send data according to their configuration,
        registration, and use. Built-in usage telemetry defaults to Lite when a
        destination and shown notice are present; desktop builds also check their
        release feed. This page summarizes the declared inventory and known gaps.
      </p>
      <p class="fine privacy-updated">Last updated {updated} · Kontour AI LLC</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">The default: what stays on your machine</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        Station is self-hosted: it runs on your own machine or on a server your
        operator runs, and keeps its workspace state in storage there.
        Accounts are optional. Full product-usage telemetry uses a
        publisher-specific monthly pseudonym derived from a random installation
        secret, not an account. Built-in Lite aggregates carry no installation
        identifier. Station
        uses the Kontour push gateway only for a phone you register, and the
        gateway forwards what it relays sealed, without the key to open it.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        Everything in the next section describes a case where data does leave.
        Destinations can come from operator settings or the build, as well as
        connected providers and registered devices. Review the choices below
        before using a networked feature.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">What can leave, and what turns it on</h2>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Your model and engine</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        When an agent runs, Station sends your messages, supported attachments,
        tool results, and selected context to the model connection or engine you
        chose. That provider is your choice, and its own handling applies to what
        it receives. A local executable can still use hosted inference or
        external tools, and their service configuration and privacy practices
        apply as well.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Desktop updates</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        A desktop build that includes an updater contacts its configured release
        feed. The inventory records that contact; it does not yet have its own
        entry describing what the request carries.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Product-usage telemetry</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Built-in telemetry has independent Off, Lite, and Full choices for each
        publisher, defaulting to Lite. Lite sends identifier-free daily
        aggregates for the completed previous UTC day, at most once per day per
        publisher, after a current shown-notice receipt and with a configured
        destination. Aggregates include coarse token-use buckets for
        conversations run in Station; outside CLI use is excluded. Resume
        baselines and accounting corrections can undercount usage. Conversations
        taken over from an outside Codex CLI currently contribute no tokens;
        missing usage is not a measured zero.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        Full requires that publisher's current disclosure and destination
        acknowledgement. It sends versioned events with timestamps rounded to
        the UTC minute, inventory revision, allowlisted build details, startup
        metadata, session-recovery and engine-turn outcomes, and selected plugin,
        settings, and connection observations. Its installation identifier is
        hashed separately for each publisher and rotates monthly. It is
        pseudonymous, not anonymous, and Station does not link it to an account.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        The operator destination comes from Settings or
        STATION_PRODUCT_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT (legacy alias STATION_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT).
        An independent Kontour sender requires a nonempty build-time address
        and its own notice and consent state; changing the operator endpoint
        does not disable that sender. The address is empty in source, so source
        builds send nothing to Kontour through it. A nonempty DO_NOT_TRACK value
        other than 0 forces Off for all publishers.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        Optional fixed-choice feedback is identifier-free and publisher-scoped.
        A reason for lowering a tier can send before the lower choice, including
        Off, takes effect. A separate reason requires a recorded lowering within
        ten minutes; together they are limited to one per publisher per UTC day.
        Do Not Track suppresses this feedback. A local payload ledger retains
        send attempts and grouped drops for at most 30 days and 5 MiB; inspect it
        with Station's telemetry outbox command.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Plugin publisher analytics</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Signed public-registry plugins can declare bounded events and count
        buckets for their own publisher. Plugin analytics defaults to Off and
        requires explicit opt-in; showing a notice does not enable it. Plugin
        Lite sends previous-day count histograms after its publisher notice.
        Station adds no installation identifier, but plugin-chosen counts can
        encode a stable identifier, so plugin Lite is not identifier-free.
        Plugin Full requires an explicit choice and current destination and
        inventory acknowledgement, and adds timestamps and the publisher's
        monthly installation pseudonym.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        Station sends to the signed declaration's HTTPS destination without
        operator credentials. Non-public addresses and redirects are refused.
        Receivers still see network metadata such as the source IP. Do Not Track
        forces Off. Uninstall removes pending counts and receipts; an optional
        fixed-choice uninstall reason can send before removal when permitted,
        capped at one per publisher per UTC day. The bounded local ledger records
        delivery outcomes. Distribution-manifest loading and broker acceptance
        remain deferred.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Diagnostics (OpenTelemetry)</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Performance metrics and traces. Station starts exporting them only when an
        operator configures an OpenTelemetry (OTLP) endpoint, and sends them to
        that endpoint, which is theirs, not ours. Station attaches its random
        installation hash, the operating-system type, and each instrument's
        attributes. The OpenTelemetry SDK it pins also detects environment,
        process, and host attributes by default, so what is actually exported
        depends on those defaults and on the operator's configuration. Saved
        collector changes take effect after restart. The random installation hash alone does not make the whole payload
        non-identifying: review what your configuration exports before pointing
        it anywhere.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        Until 11 August 2026 that identifier was derived from your machine's
        hostname and operating-system username, and this page said so. It is
        recorded here rather than quietly replaced, because a privacy policy
        that silently improves is one you cannot check.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Knowledge content</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Documents you add to Knowledge are stored through Station's configured
        storage. The embedding provider you select receives document chunks and
        your search queries; the vector store you select receives chunk text,
        vectors, and document metadata. Embedding choices include Amazon Bedrock,
        OpenAI-compatible, and Ollama endpoints. A local default does not stop a
        remote provider you configure from receiving that content, so review
        your provider settings before adding documents.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Private memory preview</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Private memory is a default-off preview. When enabled, an operator can
        stage content and evidence in an author-private root associated with the
        owner's identity. Pending proposals stay out of ordinary record lists
        and index rebuilds. Turning the preview off retains those proposals.
        A configured embedding provider receives search queries and, when
        existing memory records are explicitly indexed, their chunks. Index
        rebuilds do not send pending proposals to that provider.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Voice</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Microphone audio is captured only while you have explicitly started a
        voice session. The mobile app sends it over its authenticated connection
        to your own Station, which forwards it to the speech provider you
        configured. For the built-in provider, Nova Sonic, that is Amazon
        Bedrock. If you use a browser's own speech features instead, that
        browser's handling and retention are the platform's, and we cannot
        describe them for you. The voice adapter you select controls microphone
        capture and how the audio travels; nothing here certifies how every
        possible extension uses a device.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Agent activity on your phone</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        If you register a paired phone, Station can send it an agent-activity
        card: up to five sessions' titles, their project names, their status,
        and activity counts. A session title can contain text from your
        request. Before it leaves your Station, the card is encrypted with a key
        set up when the phone registered.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        It then travels through the Kontour push gateway (push.kontourai.io,
        unless your operator configures another) and on to Google Firebase Cloud
        Messaging for Android or Apple Push Notification service for an iOS Live
        Activity. The gateway forwards the sealed card and does not hold the key
        to open it. The gateway and the push provider do not see the session
        titles or project names. They do see routing data: push tokens or
        channel IDs, the registration's identity, your Station's push-key
        fingerprint, the size and timing of each delivery, and transport fields
        such as the event and alert priority. Nothing is sent without an active
        registration, and your registration and notification settings decide
        which device receives it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Notifications on your phone</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Station can also push the notifications its delivery policy selects to a
        registered paired phone, through the same gateway and push providers.
        The notification's title and body, kept to a bounded length, and its
        routing identities are encrypted with the same registration key. On
        Android the sealed payload can also carry a reference to the session and
        a retraction. On iOS an alert needs a separately registered alert token,
        and the gateway sends fixed, generic alert text chosen by the kind of
        notification; the real text travels only inside the sealed payload, so
        unless an extension on the phone opens it, iOS shows the generic text.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        If you hide notification content, the title and body are removed or
        replaced before encryption. The gateway and the push provider still see
        routing data, the urgency or alert kind, and the size and timing of each
        message. A message the gateway sent is not proof that the phone
        displayed it. Removing a phone's registration stops it being chosen for
        future pushes; it does not recall a message already sent or shown.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Notifications in a browser</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Browser push is separate from phone push, and it is off in Station's
        standard hosted setup. Where it is on and you allow it, registering the
        browser as a paired device stores that browser's push subscription
        endpoint and encryption keys with the device. Selected notifications are
        encrypted for the browser's push service; hiding content replaces the
        title and body but keeps routing and link fields, and the push service
        still sees the destination, transport metadata, timing, and size. To
        stop it, use Station's Unsubscribe action: it removes the browser's
        local subscription and tries to clear the registration on your Station,
        which can remain until a later cleanup if that attempt fails. Turning
        the feature setting off only hides the controls; it does not unsubscribe
        a browser that is already registered.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Sign-in, when accounts are on</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        When an operator enables local accounts, Station stores account and
        session information for sign-in and recovery, which can include the IP
        address and user-agent of the request. If the operator also configures
        an external (OIDC) sign-in provider, Station contacts that provider at
        startup for discovery and exchanges login and identity data with it when
        you sign in. The account, token, and session records it returns are kept
        in the operator's authentication store. With the Member passkeys preview
        enabled, Station also stores each credential's public key, ID, signature
        counter, name, and authenticator details such as its model and whether
        it is synced. It does not store a passkey private key or biometric data.
        Sign-in is separate from Device and Project authorization.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Camera</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Used for one thing: scanning a pairing code to connect a device to your
        Station. Camera frames are read only while the scanner is open, they are
        decoded locally in the app's web view, and the decoded value is used to
        pair. This scanner does not send camera imagery to a Station-operated
        third party.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">Tracking and advertising</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        None declared. Every entry in Station's privacy inventory is declared as not
        used for tracking, and the iOS privacy manifest generated from it
        declares no tracking and lists no tracking domains. Every declared
        purpose is app functionality or analytics; no entry declares an
        advertising purpose.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">How this page is kept honest</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        Station keeps a single privacy inventory in its source. Its iOS privacy
        manifest, its draft Play Data Safety answers, and its generated privacy
        policy are all rendered from that inventory. Station's tests fail if a
        committed copy of any of them drifts from what the inventory renders, or
        if a telemetry event starts collecting a property the inventory has not
        reviewed.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        This page is written by hand from that generated policy. Automated
        checks compare the reviewed policy, inventory, supporting guides, and
        this page with the bytes recorded in a review manifest. Product changes
        or later copy edits require a new review. The policy fingerprint is{' '}
        <code class="code-plain">{inventoryFingerprint}</code>, the first 16 hex
        characters of the SHA-256 of{' '}
        <a href={policyUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Station's{' '}<code class="code-plain">docs/privacy-policy.md</code></a>. Hash the
        current file yourself: if you get a different value, the inventory has
        changed since this page was written.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        The inventory's own caveats apply here too. Its tracking, linkage and
        purpose columns are declarations that people make, not conclusions the
        generator reaches. Its review read the source and pinned dependencies
        with synthetic tests; it has not exercised a real identity-provider
        sign-in, OpenTelemetry resource export, delivery to a real phone or
        browser, or a store submission. The store classifications, whether the
        inventory is complete, and each provider's own practices need separate
        review. A matching fingerprint identifies the reviewed policy version;
        it does not prove that this summary is correct or that the inventory is
        a complete account of what Station collects.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        One honest limitation: the site policy at <a href="/privacy/">/privacy/</a>{' '}
        links each statement to the source that implements it. This page does
        not. The generated policy linked above names the source files behind
        each entry, and Station's source is public under Apache-2.0, so you can
        follow them there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">Deletion, and how to reach us</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        Station's data is in its own storage on your machine or your operator's
        server, so deleting it is yours or your operator's to do. If you
        configured an endpoint that received telemetry or diagnostics, or an
        embedding or vector provider, review that receiver's deletion controls
        and retention policy too. Turning off a preview or telemetry does not
        delete existing records or recall delivered data. A configured Kontour
        or plugin-publisher destination may hold data independently of your
        operator's storage. To stop pushes to a phone, remove its registration.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        Anything else — questions, a deletion request for something we hold, or a
        correction to this page:{' '}
        <Fragment set:html="<!--email_off-->" /><a href="mailto:hello@kontourai.io">hello@kontourai.io</a><Fragment set:html="<!--email_on-->" />.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>

<style>
  .privacy-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .privacy-lead {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .privacy-updated {
    color: var(--color-chalk-3);
  }
  .privacy-h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .privacy-h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--color-chalk);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
